.TH CTPOOL_CONSTRUCT_SCOPED 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ctpool_construct_scoped \- declare, initialise, and auto-destroy a thread pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_construct_scoped(name, num_threads, queue_capacity);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Does what ctpool_declare_scoped() and the initialisation of
ctpool_construct() do, in one statement. The pool is destroyed by
itself when the variable leaves scope, with a drain shutdown first if
nothing has run one already.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name            Variable name for the pool handle.
num_threads     Number of worker threads to spawn (must be >= 1).
queue_capacity  Maximum pending tasks (0 for unbounded). The macro
                calls _ccol_fatal_err() for ccol_invalid_size, as it
                does for every other failure of the creation.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It calls _ccol_fatal_err() on a failure, so call ctpool_create()
directly when you must handle an error yourself.
It needs GCC or Clang, because it uses __attribute__((cleanup)).
The drain inside the destructor can block for a long time if the tasks
are slow, so call ctpool_shutdown_drain() or
ctpool_shutdown_immediate() yourself before the end of scope when you
need a bound on how long leaving the scope takes.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void process_batch(void) {
    ctpool_construct_scoped(pool, 4, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
        ctpool_submit(pool, work_fn, items[i], NULL);
    ctpool_shutdown_drain(pool);
}  /* pool destroyed automatically */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_construct (3),
.BR ctpool_declare_scoped (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
